WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.234
-Thank you so much
for coming back to the show.

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.000
It is so great to see you.
-It is my pleasure.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.334
-Thank you, thank you,
thank you.

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.300
-Late night is back!
-It's back! We're finally back!

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.801
Yes. It's been -- I've been
missing it, but thank you.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:12.300
This means a lot to me.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.067
The last time I saw you --

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:16.467
I don't know if you remember
this, but I have to bring it up.

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.234
-I do.
-Your daughter was on our show.

00:00:18.234 --> 00:00:19.300
-Yeah.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.133
-Maya was performing,
and she sang.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.667
And you were just here
as just a supportive mom,

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.801
and it was so cool.
-Sneaking around in the back.

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:28.133
-Yeah. And you were backstage
and just rooting her on.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.067
-Dancing.
-I loved it. But that is so --

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.367
I just loved that --
'Cause I didn't get a chance

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:34.434
to say like, "Oh, by the way,
your mom, Uma Thurman,

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:35.467
was here rooting you on."

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:36.868
I didn't want
to make a big deal.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.334
-No, she wouldn't
have liked that.

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:39.734
-She wouldn't. Yeah.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.400
But I love that you did that
backstage.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.801
And it was so charming
and very, very --

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:46.400
And you also had
your younger daughter.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.801
-Yeah.
My little one was with us.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.567
-Luna?
-It was a family affair.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:50.834
-Yeah. How old is, uh --

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.601
-Luna's 11.
-She's 11.

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:55.100
Alright. I have
a 10- and an 8-year-old.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.534
-I know.
-Yeah. So they're getting older.

00:00:56.534 --> 00:00:58.601
-Yeah, I know.
Enjoy every minute of it.

00:00:58.601 --> 00:00:59.901
-Yeah.
-Enjoy it.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.133
-It just happens. Right?
It just -- Yeah.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.234
I'm getting to that
kind of years of like,

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:05.834
"Dad, I wonder --
Like, what's a crush?

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.701
I want to have a crush
on somebody." I'm like...

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:09.434
-Yeah, you've got
very little time left,

00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:10.567
I'm afraid to warn you.

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.767
-I do, right?
-Very little time left.

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:13.901
-I know, okay? I'm loving it.

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.534
-Soak it up.
-I'm loving every single second.

00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:17.501
-This Christmas has to be
the best time of your life.

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:20.701
-Yeah. Oh, my God. Yeah, it is.
She always keeps saying --

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:22.300
-It will be, too.
-She goes,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.467
"When did you have
your first crush, Dad?"

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.434
I go, "What grade are you
in again? Fifth grade?"

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:27.934
I go, "I had mine in sixth."

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.601
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:31.267
-And then next year
when she asks me,

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.434
I'm gonna say,
"I had mine in seventh."

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:34.200
Yeah. Just keep
pushing it forward.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.300
-Just keep pushing it. You can
always put braces on them

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.300
even if they don't need them.
-Yeah. Just give it...

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.901
-Just give them braces.
-You're having braces.

00:01:40.901 --> 00:01:42.434
-Full metal head.
-That is great advice.

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:44.067
That is absolutely --
This is great advice.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.834
-Just say to the doctor,
"I know it looks fine.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:47.601
Can you put, like,
some headgear on her?"

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:49.567
-Yeah. Exactly.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.267
I want to talk about this
because -- Room to Grow.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.734
You've been with them...
-Since the beginning, yeah.

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:57.234
-25 years?
-It's an incredible story.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.934
It was actually Maya
I was pregnant with,

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.100
who's now 25.
-Wow.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.400
-And I lived in this brownstone
in Greenwich Village.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.834
And I had one neighbor,

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:08.701
this lovely, beautiful
young woman who was my age.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:09.901
And I was pregnant and, like,

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:11.601
getting all nervous
about it and like,

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:13.133
"How do I get ready
for this baby?

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.000
What am I gonna do
and what would I need?"

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.000
And then in the hallway,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.100
there kept being
all this baby stuff piling up.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.901
And I was watching my neighbor,
and she's very slender.

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:25.767
-Yeah.
-And I was becoming ginormous.

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:28.200
And I was like, "What's going on
with my neighbor?"

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.667
So finally I asked her...
-"When are you due?"

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:33.100
-I said, "What are you doing
with all this baby stuff?"

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.367
And she said, "I've got to
talk to you about it."

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:38.734
It's a serious, serious problem
in our community.

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:42.300
There are no resources
for pregnant women

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.901
and babies, 0 to 3,
in our community,

00:02:44.901 --> 00:02:47.901
and it is the most crucial time
of a child's life.

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.767
80% --
Babies are amazing, right?

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:51.467
80% of the human brain

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:54.067
is developing in the first
three years of life.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:58.033
So how we treat families,
parents, and babies

00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.701
in that crucial window

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:01.300
really affects
the rest of their life.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.868
-Yeah, and if you don't have
anything, then it's...

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:06.033
-It's just the duress.
Yeah. There's nutrition.

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:08.901
There's all the baby essentials
that you need.

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:10.167
I mean, it's crazy.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:11.968
And also Room to Grow

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:14.067
does, like, this
comprehensive wraparound

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:17.133
where, every quarter, the family
goes to see a social worker

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:18.901
and they get to ask
all the questions

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:21.634
that you need to ask as you're
developing, baby is developing.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.067
-Yeah, and coming back
into the workforce

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:25.601
and getting all that stuff.

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:27.901
-If you can. And job --
And finding

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:30.801
other resources for job training
and childcare.

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:32.734
And housing issues
are a huge issue for people.

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:34.534
-It is such a great thing
what you're doing.

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:36.968
But just -- I just want to
show some of the things that --

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:39.601
This is what a mother will get
on the --

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:41.367
This is just the first year.
-Yeah.

00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:43.300
-They will get
some of these things.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.467
That's just --

00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:46.534
-And you need all those things.

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:47.834
-That's just the first year.

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:50.567
I mean, it's so helpful.
It's so helpful.

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.834
And is it just New York
or is it...?

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:54.400
-We are in Boston now.
-Hey.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.033
-And it is my dream
to see this organization

00:03:58.033 --> 00:04:00.968
be in every city where
there is need and a community

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:04.467
that wants a good tool
to support their community.

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:06.701
-Let's have it keep growing.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:08.701
-I want to talk
about this, too.

00:04:08.701 --> 00:04:12.200
This is the giant awesome
fun bag.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.367
This is
the Donate By Mail program.

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:16.133
Okay?
-Yeah.

00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:19.267
-And so can you explain
what this is and
where do you get this?

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:21.934
-Okay, so, since we see
the family every quarter --

00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:24.534
and as we all know,
babies change radically

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:26.968
like every few months
and you need new stuff,

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.667
so you barely use the stuff
that you had

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.467
before you need new stuff.
-Yeah.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:31.934
-So it's really lightly used.

00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:35.167
So a family with a baby --

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:38.133
95% of our things
are recycled, upcycled.

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:40.133
So a family with a baby

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:42.000
just packs up the stuff
they don't need anymore

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.601
when they're getting their new
stuff, put it in the bag,

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:46.968
and it gets shipped
back to Room to Grow.

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:50.601
-Yeah. If you go
to roomtogrow.org,

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:53.400
you get the bag.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.634
I think the bag is $15.

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:57.100
-It's a commitment of $15.
-$15.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.000
You get the bag sent to you.
-And then it's shipped back.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.234
-And then it already has the
shipping label and everything,

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:02.901
so all you have to do
is fill it with stuff

00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:04.200
that you want to donate,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.801
so if you have anything --
Books Like you said --

00:05:06.801 --> 00:05:09.234
-Books are really important.
English language.

00:05:09.234 --> 00:05:11.334
Spanish language.
Very important also.

00:05:11.334 --> 00:05:14.267
Socks, gloves, winter coats,
all the things that you need.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:15.567
-All that stuff.

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:17.534
And I always bring this up
every single time,

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:19.701
but I remember
when you first had Maya.

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:21.300
-Yeah.
-And you told me, like,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.467
"Jimmy, if you're
lucky enough to have kids,

00:05:23.467 --> 00:05:25.234
you have to read to them."
-Yes.

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:26.601
-And you were reading
"Ferdinand," by the way,

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:28.067
to Maya.
-I was reading "Ferdinand."

00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:29.534
You do have to read
to your children.

00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:31.834
And that's another
crucial thing.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:33.334
That kind of intervention --

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:37.000
giving a family the right books
to read to their child.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.467
-Yeah.
-Like, if you don't have

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:41.000
that book, how can you
do that for your child?

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.467
-Yeah. This is great.
Roomtogrow.org, everybody.

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:46.267
Thank you so much
for coming on.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:47.734
-I love you.
-And you know you're the best.

00:05:47.734 --> 00:05:49.234
Uma Thurman, everybody!

00:05:49.234 --> 00:05:51.467
Room to Grow's
25th Anniversary Gala

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:53.067
is October 25th

00:05:53.067 --> 00:05:55.133
at the Ziegfeld Ballroom
in New York City.

00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:57.100
Lil Rel Howery joins us
after the break.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.300
Stick around, everybody!

